        ** DISEASE

             Don't drink cave water unless you are DESPERATELY thirsty.
        Most cave water comes relatively quickly from the surface and is
        loaded with all sorts of germs.

             Many people go caving where there are large numbers (millions)
        of bats.  While bats won't bother you directly, when their are so
        many of them around you risk picking up something indirectly:
        histoplasmosis is a fungal lung disease found in bat guano which is
        unpleasant but generally not fatal; bats have fleas, and large
        numbers of bats drop large numbers of fleas with all their
        associated diseases; rabies may be caught, but this is unproven and
        certainly very rare.  This type of caving is strictly at your own
        risk.

        ** GETTING LOST

             As you go through a cave, look around to familiarize yourself
        with the surroundings.  Most people get disoriented sooner or
        later.  The best way to solve the problem is to backtrack and look
        carefully at things.  Whenever you emerge from a small hole into a
        larger space, take a good look at the hole before moving on.
        Whenever you come to an intersection and must make a choice as to
        which way to go, proceed a short distance, turn around and remember
        the intersection and the way you came in.  Taking these steps is
        generally enough to make you recall your path when you are
        backtracking or exiting the cave.  The sight of something you have
        seen before is generally enough to make you remember.  Also, giving
        names to landmarks is a good way to remember them.

             Stay within earshot of your buddy.  A caving group should
        never break up into single people.  Sometimes a person will go down
        a hole to "take a look".  In this case someone else should remain
        by the hole and an agreed upon time limit should be set for the
        explorer's return.  This should never be more than a few minutes.
        When travelling in a large group, it is important that the leaders
        go slowly enough so that the people on the end don't become
        separated.  Each person must stay within earshot of the persons
        ahead and behind.

             Finally, if you do get lost and backtracking doesn't help,
        STAY PUT.  Try to make yourself comfortable and stay warm and dry.
        Wait for someone to find you.  It is important that someone outside
        the cave or at home know where you are and about when you are to
        return.  That person is your final safeguard.

             When you come out of a cave make sure everyone in your group
        is accounted for.  At your earliest convenience, call home and tell
        them that everyone is out and you are on your way back.
